 ## Overview
-Quadric Error Metric Tetrahedral Simplification is a mesh decimation algorithm that reduces the vertex count of a tetrahedral mesh by iteratively collapsing edges, minimizing geometric and field approximation errors using quadric metrics [S2]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by supporting volumetric (tetrahedral) meshes rather than just surface triangles, and by incorporating a positional correction term to ensure numerical stability during collapse operations [S1, S2]. It builds on [GPU-based Mesh Decimation via Edge Collapse](3505-gpu-based-mesh-decimation-via-edge-collapse.md) but extends the error metric to handle 3D volume elements and scalar field data.
+Quadric Error Metric Tetrahedral Simplification is a mesh decimation algorithm that reduces the vertex count of a tetrahedral mesh by iteratively collapsing edges, minimizing geometric and field approximation errors using quadric metrics [S2]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by supporting volumetric (tetrahedral) meshes rather than just surface triangles, and by incorporating a positional correction term to ensure numerical stability during collapse operations [S1, S2]. It builds on the edge collapse paradigm [3505-gpu-based-mesh-decimation-via-edge-collapse.md] but extends the error metric to handle 3D volume elements and scalar field data [G].
 
 ## How it works
